// @no-instantiate
// @hint redundant type annotation: `f` is inferred as `i32` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `q` is inferred as `{b: string, a: i32}` — remove it to lean on inference
// XFAIL-MISCOMPILE (the kind's first member; see README.vl). This program is WELL-TYPED and
// the checker is RIGHT to accept it — the 2026-08 ruling is that `{a: i32, b: string}` and
// `{b: string, a: i32}` are THE SAME TYPE, field order carrying no meaning for an ordinary
// object type. `assignable` agrees and takes `Q` where `P` is asked for.
//
// The EMITTER does not. `shapeRowSetId` walks a row's fields POSITIONALLY (`sFieldStart[si]
// + i`), so two field-permuted twins hash to different ids, intern as different rows, and
// emit two distinct wasm struct types:
//
//   (type 0 (struct (field (mut i32))     (field (mut (ref 2)))))   ;; P
//   (type 1 (struct (field (mut (ref 2))) (field (mut i32))))       ;; Q
//
// `vl check --codegen` exits 0 and `vl run` reports
// `type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)`. Pre-existing on master.
//
// IT IS ONLY THE **NAMED** DECLARATION THAT FAILS. The identical permutation written INLINE
// works — `const q: { b: string, a: i32 } = …` passed to `f(x: P)` runs, and the module
// carries ONE struct row rather than two.
//
// THE MECHANISM, PRECISELY (an earlier note in this file said named declarations "bypass a
// lookup"; they do not consult one, and neither does the same-order case — the two are saved
// by different things):
//
//   · An INLINE annotation resolves through `annShapeIndexOf`, which is ORDER-INSENSITIVE:
//     it buckets on `shapeFieldSetIdOf` (which SORTS the field sids) and then matches with
//     `sFieldIndex(si, names[i])` — a lookup BY FIELD NAME, not by position. So a permuted
//     inline spelling finds `P`'s existing row and no second row is ever minted.
//   · A NAMED declaration mints unconditionally — `sNames.push(s.tdName)` for a `TypeDecl`,
//     `internShapeAs` for the struct-alias union form. Neither consults `annShapeIndexOf`.
//     So `P` and `Q` are always two ROWS.
//   · Two rows collapse to one wasm HEAP TYPE only when their field SEQUENCE is identical.
//     That is why three same-order aliases emit ONE struct type and run, and why the
//     permuted pair emits two and fails validation. The merge is positional; the row lookup
//     is not.
//
// So the order-insensitive machinery exists and is proven by the inline path; the named path
// simply never reaches it.
//
// Measured across 14 positions: param, binding, return, array element, struct field, map
// value, generic argument, nullable, `.push`, closure parameter and an all-i32 permutation
// are ALL check-clean invalid wasm. Only three work — the same-order inline, the PERMUTED
// inline, and boxing into a union.
//
// THE `is` QUESTION IS ALREADY ANSWERED BY EXISTING BEHAVIOUR, which an earlier note here
// treated as open. Two aliases with the SAME field order already share a row and already
// share an `is` verdict — `type P = {a:i32}` / `type Q = {a:i32}`, a `Q` value boxed into
// `P | i32`, and `u is P` is TRUE. So "structurally identical named aliases are
// indistinguishable by `is`" is not a new semantic this fix would introduce; it is what the
// compiler already does, and extending it to permuted twins is the consistent completion.
//
// What remains is therefore a straightforward choice of SITE, not of semantics:
//   (a) make the named declaration consult `annShapeIndexOf` before minting, so the twins
//       become one ROW — smallest change, reuses the order-insensitive path the inline
//       spelling already proves, and the heap-type merge then follows for free;
//   (b) make the heap-type merge itself order-insensitive — larger, and it would have to
//       carve out the FLAT types, whose offsets ARE their layout.
//
// The FLAT-type exception is not at risk either way: `flat type` layout is pinned by
// `tests/cases/memory/flat-layout-grid.vl` ("order is layout, and nothing is reordered to
// pack better") and is a separate declaration form from the object types this is about.
// THE SECOND HINT IS THE BUG SAYING ITS OWN NAME: the checker reports `q` as inferred
// `{b: string, a: i32}` — the PERMUTED spelling — and still accepts it where `P` is asked
// for, because to the checker they are one type. The emitter reads the same two spellings
// and mints two rows.
type P = { a: i32, b: string }

type Q = { b: string, a: i32 }

function f(x: P): i32 {
  x.a
}

const q: Q = { b: "s", a: 7 }

print(f(q))
